# Day 41 - 4Sum

## Problem Statement
Given an integer array `nums` of `n` integers and an integer `target`, return all the unique quadruplets `[nums[a], nums[b], nums[c], nums[d]]` such that:

- `0 <= a, b, c, d < n`
- `a`, `b`, `c`, and `d` are distinct indices.
- `nums[a] + nums[b] + nums[c] + nums[d] == target`

The solution set must not contain duplicate quadruplets.

## Code

class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> fourSum(vector<int>& arr, int target) {
        int n = arr.size();
        sort(arr.begin(), arr.end());

        vector<vector<int>> ans;

        for (int i = 0; i < n - 3; i++) {
            if (i > 0 && arr[i] == arr[i - 1])
                continue;

            for (int j = i + 1; j < n - 2; j++) {
                if (j > i + 1 && arr[j] == arr[j - 1])
                    continue;

                int k = j + 1, l = n - 1;

                while (k < l) {
                    long long sum =
                        (long long)arr[i] + arr[j] + arr[k] + arr[l];

                    if (sum < target) {
                        k++;
                    }
                    else if (sum > target) {
                        l--;
                    }
                    else {
                        ans.push_back({arr[i], arr[j], arr[k], arr[l]});
                        k++;
                        l--;

                        while (k < l && arr[k] == arr[k - 1])
                            k++;

                        while (k < l && arr[l] == arr[l + 1])
                            l--;
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
};




## Time Complexity
**O(n³)**

## Space Complexity
**O(1)** (excluding the output list)

> The space required to store the resulting quadruplets is not counted as auxiliary space.

## Approach
This solution first sorts the array so that the **Two Pointers** technique can be applied efficiently. Two nested loops fix the first two elements of the quadruplet, while two pointers (`k` and `l`) search for the remaining two elements whose sum equals the target. If the current sum is smaller than the target, the left pointer is moved forward; if it is larger, the right pointer is moved backward. When a valid quadruplet is found, it is added to the answer, and duplicate values are skipped for all pointers to ensure only unique quadruplets are returned. A `long long` is used while calculating the sum to prevent integer overflow when adding four integers.


